output': 'Evaluation: There are two main reasons to pass here. First, card counting reveals that all Aces are accounted for (Next played a pair of Aces in Turn 6, and you hold the other two). This means your pair of Aces is the highest pair below 2s, a very strong defensive structure. Breaking this pair to play a single Ace would significantly weaken your hand. Second, the Next player (Right) has only 3 cards left and is a major threat. The Previous player (Left) has played a King, which blocks the Next player unless they use a 2, a Joker, or a Bomb (since no other Aces exist). By passing, you force the dangerous Next player to either deplete their high-value cards or lose the turn, while preserving your 2 and strong pairs (QQ, AA) for the endgame.\n\nReason: All Kings and Aces are accounted for (you played KKK earlier, Left played K; Right played AA, you have AA). Left\'s King forces Right (3 cards left) to play a 2 or Joker to win the trick. Passing preserves your strong pairs (QQ, AA) and your control card (2), while forcing the opponents to exhaust their top cards.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": []}'}
